{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HamziTpCzxc5YDPLAsetNV","type":"tool_result","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-7; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding. Every single_solve so far was ONE layer.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, 18, 23, 28, 33). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: TT about 5-7 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1), mastery about 5-7 s from day 2 on, 9 s on day 7 (grows slowly with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, ...); detect\n  each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order changes).\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day (33 on day 7); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`, top-level\n  `sha256` = the zip). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (318 vs 333 bytes); same sentence and encoding.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n67:**Day 7: Percent Chance** (\"My address bar is having a moment\").\n68-- 318-byte blob, every byte percent-encoded (`%4e%69...`), ONE layer -> `urllib.parse.unquote` -> slug. Start->CORRECT 53 s, first submit.\n69-- Worker wrote the solver from the harness skeleton (alphabet-detected `decode_frag`: `%xx` -> binary -> dec/oct -> hex -> b64 -> rot13).\n70-- TT window 7 s, CORRECT at 2.4 s. Mastery 33 sets x 7 lines (+ url line), window 9 s.\n71-- Mastery attempt 1 failed: set1 resolved to the WHOLE zip (bundle saved as `blob-01.txt`, a name match returned the outer file), so unquote over all 33 members gave another set's slug fragment (`py-storm-dune-80` from set 27's `zippy-...`) -> incorrect; set26 then `expired`. After `lib.common.find_artifact` was patched to return the same-named member, attempt 2 was CORRECT (7.1 s of 9 s; the start call alone took 2.9 s).\n72-\n--\n81:- A `.txt` file that is really a ZIP (check the magic, not the extension). Prefer the exact inner member over the outer same-named container. Day 7 mastery lost an attempt to this: set 1 decoded the whole bundle. A slug that is a word suffix (`py-...`), or a set1 answer that doesn't come from its own last line, is the tell. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the zip-named-`blob-01.txt` layout, so test on the raw delivery.\n82-- English scoring: count single letters and hex reads as English (day 3 timeout). Score common 2-letter words without a margin and a correct line gets rot-shifted (day 4). Require a margin over the unshifted text.\n83-- Encoding confusion: binary is also valid hex (try binary first), and decimal is also valid octal (use padding and case).\n84-- Fragments are split mid-word. Join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace. Joining with spaces corrupts the sentence and the slug.\n85-- No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Use python or `tr ' ' '\\n'` plus an explicit newline. Always regex-check the slug against the answer syntax.\n86-- Timed windows (5-9 s, with GM start/submit eating 3-5 s of it) leave no room for an LLM or a slow BFS. Keep every per-question decode under about 1 s with a hard BFS cap.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KZJDizkK7MXeyshtQBkfKu","agent_id":"ae8546f4cf49d0350","session_id":"a2624e74-c74a-41db-9965-6b2cb921c121","uuid":"16d08c56-7c03-45b4-bc1a-090eec659cd1","timestamp":"2026-10-07T13:08:29.951Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"warmups day07 technique log"}
